GENAI TASK PLANNING AND AUTONOMOUS COLLABORATION
Project implementation guide | 1
PROJECT IMPLEMENTATION GUIDE
Generative-AI-Enabled Task Planning and
Dynamic Allocation for Heterogeneous 
Autonomous Agents
A rigorous, step-by-step guide from problem definition to validated simulation and 
demonstration
Core design principle
Generative AI translates a high-level mission into a proposed structured task plan. Deterministic validators 
check that plan. A separate allocator assigns tasks, a scheduler manages dependencies, and a safety layer 
controls what may execute. The language model never sends unchecked low-level movement commands.
What this guide enables you to build
 A reproducible Python simulation of several autonomous agents with different capabilities.
 An LLM-based mission decomposer that emits structured tasks and dependency graphs.
 A capability-aware task allocation optimizer and a simpler baseline for comparison.
 Independent plan validation, route checking, collision prevention and event-driven replanning.
 A repeatable experiment suite with baselines, metrics, fault injection, results and a demonstrable prototype.
Recommended implementation sequence
Build the non-LLM system first. Only integrate the language model after task execution, allocation and safety 
checks work with a manually authored mission. This isolates errors and ensures the prototype is testable even 
when the model/API is unavailable.
Scope: simulation-first project. A ROS 2/Gazebo integration is an optional extension after the core system passes its acceptance tests.

