GENAI TASK PLANNING AND AUTONOMOUS COLLABORATION
Project implementation guide | 3
Part A - Formal project definition
1. Problem statement
Given a mission expressed in natural language, a finite set of heterogeneous agents, a map, a library of 
permitted task types and system constraints, the system must: (i) decompose the mission into atomic tasks; (ii) 
encode dependencies and completion criteria; (iii) validate the task graph; (iv) assign tasks to capable agents; (v)
schedule and execute feasible routes; (vi) respond to changes or failures; and (vii) record enough evidence to 
evaluate performance.
2. Concrete reference scenario
Use a neutral inspection mission as the end-to-end example. The mission is: "Inspect zones A, B and C, take the 
required readings at each zone, and compile a report after all inspections are complete." The world contains 
named locations and travel edges. Agents differ in sensing capabilities, energy levels and starting locations. Task
completion is simulated using known rules, not inferred from the LLM.
Agent Capabilities Example limitation
agent_1 visual_inspection, navigation, reporting Cannot take environmental readings
agent_2 environmental_reading, navigation Cannot perform visual inspection
agent_3 visual_inspection, 
environmental_reading, navigation, 
reporting
Starts farther away or has lower battery
3. Functional requirements
ID Requirement
FR-01 Accept a mission request and a known world/agent snapshot.
FR-02 Generate a structured task list with unique IDs, task types, 
locations, requirements, dependencies, priorities and 
completion criteria.
FR-03 Reject malformed, unsupported, cyclic, infeasible or out-ofworld task plans.
FR-04 Assign every executable task to an eligible agent or return a 
clear infeasibility reason.
FR-05 Respect task dependencies when scheduling execution.
FR-06 Find feasible paths and prohibit unsafe occupancy conflicts in 
the discrete simulator.
FR-07 Execute simulated task effects and track state transitions.
FR-08 On an agent failure or blocked route, preserve valid completed 
work and replan pending work.
FR-09 Log inputs, plans, assignments, events, state transitions, metrics 
and model metadata.
FR-10 Compare the LLM-based pipeline against non-LLM and nonoptimized baselines.

GENAI TASK PLANNING AND AUTONOMOUS COLLABORATION
Project implementation guide | 4
4. Non-functional requirements
 Reproducibility: fixed simulator seeds, versioned configuration, saved mission inputs and saved LLM 
responses.
 Fail-closed behavior: if validation or allocation fails, do not execute the proposed plan.
 Observability: every task state transition and replan decision has a timestamp and reason.
 Modularity: planner, validator, allocator, scheduler, simulator and evaluator are separate modules.
 Bounded cost: cap LLM retries, input size, output tokens and calls per mission.
 Testability: core test suite runs without calling an external LLM API by using a mocked planner response.
5. Freeze the MVP assumptions
 A single central coordinator maintains the authoritative mission and agent state.
 The initial simulator uses a graph/grid rather than continuous robot dynamics.
 Only whitelisted task types and named world locations may be produced by the planner.
 The task allocator chooses robot-task assignments; the LLM does not assign robots.
 A task completion result comes from the simulator/task executor, not from a model-generated assertion.
 For the MVP, movement is discrete and collision conflicts mean same-node occupancy or two agents 
swapping along the same edge in the same tick.
Exit gate
Write a one-page specification containing the mission example, agent capabilities, allowed task types, 
success criteria and explicit assumptions. If you cannot decide what counts as task success or a collision in 
the simulator, the problem is not yet specified well enough to code.

