Control flow for one mission
1. Parse the request and attach a mission ID, world version and current-state snapshot.
2. Ask the LLM for a candidate task graph using strict output structure and a limited task vocabulary.
3. Validate syntax, field types, allowed task types/locations, dependency references, acyclicity and 
completeness against the mission checklist.
4. Compute path and capability costs; run the allocator. If there is no feasible assignment, return an 
explanation or request mission clarification.
5. Schedule only tasks whose prerequisites are complete. Before every movement/action, run the safety 
monitor.
6. Update simulator state and log each transition. On a material event, freeze unsafe dispatch, update state and
replan only the unfinished portion.
7. At the end, compute completion, cost, timing, safety and reliability metrics from logs.
State ownership
The coordinator owns authoritative task state and assignments. The simulator owns true agent position, battery 
and task outcomes. The LLM only proposes task specifications. Do not allow the language model to overwrite 
observed state or claim that an action succeeded.
Architecture test
Replace the LLM with a deterministic mock response. The validator, allocator, scheduler, simulator and 
evaluator should still run end to end. If they cannot, too much logic has been coupled to the language-model 
call.

