What to do next: construct the simulated world and agent state using only these validated data types.
Phase 3 - Build a deterministic multi-agent world simulator
Estimated focused effort: 6-10 focused hours
Goal
Represent the world, routes, agent movement, energy and task effects without physical hardware.
Do this in order
1. Represent the map as a weighted graph. Nodes are intersections or named zones; edges have travel time and
energy cost. Use a small map with a few alternate routes so that a blocked edge changes the best path.
2. Implement a world service that returns valid neighbors, tests whether an edge is traversable and computes 
shortest path/cost. Use non-negative edge weights. For a grid, Manhattan distance may be a heuristic for 
A*; for the first version, NetworkX shortest paths are sufficient.
3. Create an AgentState object with current_node, battery, capabilities, status, active_task and route. Agent 
motion should advance one edge or one discrete move per simulation tick.
4. Implement travel energy consumption and define behavior when battery would cross the reserve: refuse 
dispatch, divert to charge, or raise LOW_BATTERY. Do not allow negative battery values.
5. Implement the task executor as deterministic simulated effects. For example, visual_inspection records a 
synthetic observation for a zone; environmental_reading records a seeded numeric reading; 
compile_report creates a report only after required task results exist.
6. Create a fixed-step simulation loop: process queued events; update eligible tasks; request action proposals; 
check safety; advance agents; check task postconditions; emit state-transition logs.
7. Keep randomness controlled by a seed. The same config and seed should reproduce the same world, event 
sequence and task results.
Deliverable to create
Working simulator module with world, agents, routes, energy, task effects and a single-seed end-to-end unit 
scenario.
Exit gate - do not move on until this is true
With a manually authored plan, agents can move to target nodes, spend energy, perform tasks and record 
results. Invalid routes and insufficient battery are handled explicitly. A second run with the same seed 
produces the same trace.
What to do next: Add deterministic task scheduling and a simple non-LLM mission execution path in Phase 4.

