Project implementation guide | 10
Phase 4 - Implement dependencies, scheduling and 
deterministic execution
Estimated focused effort: 4-7 focused hours
Goal
Prove that the system can execute a valid manually authored task graph before introducing an LLM.
Do this in order
1. Implement a dependency resolver. A task is eligible only if every task in depends_on is SUCCEEDED. Detect 
cycles at validation time rather than waiting for execution to stall.
2. At each scheduler cycle, identify pending tasks whose prerequisites succeeded. Assign or dispatch only 
eligible tasks and available agents; keep the remaining tasks waiting.
3. Implement task handlers through a finite dispatch table keyed by task_type. Each handler has explicit 
preconditions and postconditions. Never map LLM text directly to Python function names or shell 
commands.
4. Add stable task-result records containing task_id, agent_id, result payload, start/end timestamps, status and 
failure reason. A report task should consume validated earlier results.
5. Create a manually authored demo plan in a fixture file. Execute it repeatedly and verify state transitions and
postconditions.
6. Add simple wait/timeout handling. If a task cannot start because a dependency or agent is unavailable, 
record the reason rather than leaving it silently stuck.
Deliverable to create
A deterministic execution engine and baseline scenario that needs no LLM and makes all state transitions 
visible.
Exit gate - do not move on until this is true
A hand-written valid plan completes all mandatory tasks in the reference mission. An unsatisfied 
dependency blocks dispatch; a cycle is rejected before execution; task success is not recorded until its 
postcondition passes.
What to do next: Implement and measure assignment costs in Phase 5. Keep this deterministic mission as the 
regression fixture.

