Project implementation guide | 11
Phase 5 - Path costs, task allocation and assignment baselines
Estimated focused effort: 6-10 hours. This phase makes allocation decisions measurable and ensures capability 
constraints are enforced mathematically.
5.1 Build a cost matrix
For every pending task j and agent i, compute a feasibility flag and a cost c_ij. A task is infeasible for an agent if 
the required capabilities are not a subset of the agent capabilities, the route is blocked, or predicted energy use 
violates the reserve. Do not represent infeasibility only as a large cost if that could still be selected; fix the 
variable bounds or omit the assignment variable.
c_ij = w_d * travel_distance_ij
 + w_t * travel_time_ij
 + w_e * predicted_energy_ij
 + w_l * projected_workload_i
 + w_p * priority_penalty_ij
subject to:
 x_ij in {0, 1}
 each task j: sum_i x_ij = 1
 x_ij = 0 if agent i is infeasible for task j
 optional workload limit for agent i:
 sum_j duration_j * x_ij <= workload_limit_i
minimize: sum_i sum_j c_ij * x_ij
Here x_ij = 1 means task j is assigned to agent i. Choose weights before evaluation and record them in 
configuration. Normalize cost components or document their units; otherwise one numeric component may 
dominate merely because its scale is larger.
5.2 Implement two allocators
 Greedy baseline: repeatedly assign the next eligible task to the currently feasible agent with the lowest 
incremental cost. Document tie-breaking and dependency treatment.
 Optimization allocator: use a binary linear assignment model with a solver such as scipy.optimize.milp. 
Include each-task-assigned-once constraints and eligibility bounds. Add workload limits only if they 
represent a real project requirement.
 Run the same tasks and current state through both allocators; log assignment, feasibility and objective value.
Treat the optimizer as optimal only for the mathematical model encoded, not for the full route-sequencing 
problem unless sequence decisions are explicitly modelled.
 When the model is infeasible, return the unresolved tasks and their blocking reasons: no capable agent, no 
reachable path, battery limit or workload limit.
5.3 Avoid a common modelling mistake
The simple assignment objective uses distance from the current position to each task and can underestimate 
travel when one agent receives multiple tasks. Call it a static assignment approximation. Improve it later by 
using route-aware incremental cost or a joint task-sequencing model; do not label the initial version globally 
optimal for multi-robot routing.
Deliverable and exit gate
Deliverable: greedy and optimization allocators, cost matrix logs and tests for capability mismatch, 
disconnected routes, low battery and infeasible tasks. Exit gate: no infeasible assignment is selected; every 
feasible task is assigned exactly once; repeated identical inputs yield identical assignments under deterministic 

