Project implementation guide | 17
3. Compare meaningful baselines
Use the same world, mission, agent starting states and seed for paired comparisons. Do not compare methods on
different scenario difficulty and attribute the difference to the algorithm.
Variant Planner Allocator Replanning Question answered
A Manually authored tasks Greedy Off for nominal scenario Reference for execution 
and allocator cost
B LLM task decomposition Greedy Off for nominal scenario What changes when tasks
come from the LLM?
C LLM task decomposition Optimization Off for nominal scenario Does the assignment 
optimizer improve 
measured cost?
D LLM task decomposition Optimization On for fault scenarios Can the system recover 
when the state changes?
Do not infer that the LLM is better merely because Variant B completes one mission. Evaluate task-graph 
validity and requirement coverage over a fixed suite of varied mission prompts. Use saved responses for 
deterministic component tests, and live calls only for an explicitly logged planner-evaluation set.
4. Metric definitions
Metric Definition / implementation Interpretation caveat
Mission success rate Successful missions / total missions, where 
success means all mandatory tasks satisfy 
postconditions.
Report denominator and failure reasons.
Task completion rate Mandatory tasks succeeded / mandatory tasks 
in the validated mission.
Do not count tasks omitted by an incomplete 
plan as completed.
Plan validity rate LLM candidates accepted by all schema and 
semantic checks / all candidates evaluated.
Also report rejection categories and repair 
attempts.
Mission makespan Time from mission acceptance to terminal 
mission state.
Use simulated time for system performance; 
report LLM wall-clock latency separately.
Total travel distance Sum of all traversed edge distances across 
agents.
Use the same world weights across all variants.
Energy consumed Sum of simulated edge and task energy use. Document the energy model; it is a proxy, not a 
battery physics model.
Allocation objective Value of the cost function defined for 
assignment.
Only compare when weights and cost definition
are identical.
Replan recovery time Time from recorded event to first valid resumed
dispatch, or to mission termination.
Report unrecovered cases separately.
Safety violations Count of forbidden-node entry, same-node 
conflicts, edge-swap conflicts or policybypassing dispatches.
Zero observed violations is evidence only for 
tested scenarios.
Planner latency/cost Measured request latency and providerreported tokens/cost if available.Report model/version, retries and failure cases.
5. Experimental protocol
1. Create a versioned configuration file for each scenario. Fix the map, mission, agent properties and weights 
before the comparison.

GENAI TASK PLANNING AND AUTONOMOUS COLLABORATION
Project implementation guide | 18
2. Run the deterministic variants on the same set of seeds. Use at least 30 independent seeds for a first 
statistical comparison if the simulator has stochastic events; use more when confidence intervals remain 
too wide.
3. For LLM planner evaluation, use a fixed mission prompt set with paraphrases and coverage labels. Record 
the raw output and model metadata. Treat model nondeterminism as part of the measured behavior, not 
as noise to hide.
4. Report mean and standard deviation for continuous metrics, success proportion for binary outcomes and 
confidence intervals. For paired runs, analyze within-seed differences.
5. Use bootstrap confidence intervals if the metric distribution is skewed or if convenient. State the resampling
unit (whole run/seed), number of resamples and confidence level.
6. Include failure examples and invalid-plan cases. Do not drop runs that timed out, returned malformed JSON 
or failed to recover; classify them explicitly.
7. Keep the final test set separate from tuning if you adjust cost weights, prompts or thresholds using 
experiment results.
Interpretation rule
A strong result is not simply a higher score. Explain the trade-off: for example, a lower travel cost may 
increase planning latency, or more conservative safety checks may reduce throughput. Never claim 
causation or statistical significance without the matching experimental design and analysis.

