A realistic part-time target is roughly 2-3 weeks when working independently or 1-2 weeks with effective AI 
assistance. Reserve additional time for unexpected integration issues. Do not compress the validation and 
evaluation phases just to meet a date.
Optional extension - ROS 2 and Gazebo
Only start after the simulator-first project passes all exit gates. A ROS 2 extension increases integration 
complexity and should be treated as a separate milestone.
1. Choose a specific ROS 2 distribution supported by your operating system and install it in a clean 
environment. Record the distribution and package versions.
2. Map each simulated agent to a namespace and define standard interfaces for status, task command, task 
result and fault events. Use typed messages/actions rather than free-form text commands.
3. Keep the LLM/task graph/allocator at the mission layer. Delegate navigation to an established navigation 
stack or a controlled robot-specific interface. Add a hard gate between mission task dispatch and robot 
actions.
4. Use Gazebo with a small world, two or three agents, static obstacles and reproducible task areas. First 
validate one agent, then multiple agents, then add blocked routes and failure recovery.
5. Log real ROS timestamps and simulation timestamps, QoS settings and message drops. Validate state 
synchronization and stale-message handling.
6. Keep physical deployment explicitly out of scope until hardware-specific collision avoidance, emergency 
stop, watchdog, battery failsafes and operational safety reviews are designed and tested.
Extension exit gate
Demonstrate that the ROS 2 version preserves the same high-level safety rules: the LLM emits task 
specifications only, invalid plans are rejected, task dependencies are checked, and every low-level action is 
mediated by a deterministic robot interface.

